# Component Decomposition

Large, monolithic components (React, Vue, Svelte, etc.) that manage state for multiple entities or concerns become difficult to maintain, test, and reason about. Decompose them into smaller, focused components.

## Rules

1. **A component should have a single responsibility.** If it manages state for multiple unrelated entities, split it.
2. **Target a maximum of ~200-300 lines per component file** (excluding imports and types). If a component exceeds this, it is a candidate for decomposition.
3. **Extract sections into dedicated components** that manage their own hooks, state, and data fetching. The parent component becomes a layout coordinator.
4. **Colocate hooks with the component that uses them.** If a hook is only used by `SectionA`, it should not be called in the parent and passed down as props.
5. **Prefer composition over prop drilling.** If you are passing 5+ props just to thread data from parent to grandchild, consider a context provider or moving the data fetch closer to where it is consumed.

## Examples

```tsx
// BAD — monolithic root component
function AppRoot() {
  const properties = useProperties();
  const methods = useMethods();
  const results = useResults();
  const [activeTab, setActiveTab] = useState('properties');
  // 400+ lines managing three unrelated concerns...

  return (
    <div>
      <PropertiesTable data={properties.data} onUpdate={properties.update} />
      <MethodsTable data={methods.data} onUpdate={methods.update} />
      <ResultsTable data={results.data} />
    </div>
  );
}

// GOOD — decomposed into focused sections
function AppRoot() {
  return (
    <div>
      <PropertiesSection />
      <MethodsSection />
      <ResultsSection />
    </div>
  );
}

function PropertiesSection() {
  const { data, update, isLoading, error } = useProperties();
  if (isLoading) return <PropertiesSkeleton />;
  if (error) return <ErrorState error={error} />;
  return <PropertiesTable data={data} onUpdate={update} />;
}
```

```tsx
// BAD — excessive prop drilling
<GrandParent>
  <Parent userName={name} userRole={role} userPermissions={perms} onAction={handler}>
    <Child userName={name} userRole={role} userPermissions={perms} onAction={handler} />
  </Parent>
</GrandParent>

// GOOD — context for cross-cutting concerns
<UserProvider value={{ name, role, permissions }}>
  <GrandParent>
    <Parent>
      <Child /> {/* reads from useUserContext() */}
    </Parent>
  </GrandParent>
</UserProvider>
```
